            { label: 'Limit the position to 5% of your portfolio. Recognize that feeling "extremely confident" is itself a red flag, Slovic proved that confidence exceeds accuracy. Use a position-sizing rule that doesn\'t depend on confidence level.', consequence: 'Outstanding discipline. You recognized that confidence is not correlated with accuracy (Slovic), that conviction is the enemy of diversification (Archegos), and that systematic position-sizing rules protect against overconfidence better than willpower. A 5% cap means even if you\'re wrong, your portfolio survives.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'Put 20% in, a compromise between conviction and caution', consequence: 'Compromising with overconfidence still gives it too much influence. 20% in a single small-cap is concentrated by any professional standard. Position-sizing should be systematic, not negotiated with your feelings.', score: 1 }
