            { label: 'Reassess the thesis objectively: ask "Would I buy this stock today with fresh money at this price?" If no, sell. The sunk cost and your ego are irrelevant, only the forward-looking thesis matters.', consequence: 'The "fresh money" test defeats overconfidence by removing your emotional attachment. If you wouldn\'t buy the stock today, you shouldn\'t hold it, regardless of your past analysis or ego. This framework turns every hold decision into a buy decision, bypassing the disposition effect and overconfidence simultaneously.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'Average down, buy more at the lower price to reduce your cost basis', consequence: 'Averaging down on a losing position is often overconfidence doubling down. It only works if your thesis is correct, which a 30% decline suggests it may not be. You\'re increasing concentration in a losing position, the exact pattern that destroyed Archegos.', score: 1 }
