    cq('Terrance Odean\'s UC Berkeley study of 66,465 brokerage accounts found that the most active traders earned 6.5% less annually than the least active. Additionally, the stocks active traders sold outperformed the stocks they bought by:',
      ['About 0.5% over the next year, a gap so small it is essentially just noise and trading costs','About 1.0% over the next year, a modest gap mostly explained by the commissions active traders paid','3.3% over the next year, meaning traders would have earned more by doing the exact opposite of every trade','The stocks they bought actually outperformed, showing that active traders did pick the better names'],
      2,'Odean found a 3.3% reverse gap, stocks traders sold outperformed stocks they bought. This means every trade was not just costly (commissions), it was systematically wrong. The overconfident belief that "I know something the market doesn\'t" was disproved by 66,465 accounts worth of data. Active traders would have literally performed better by flipping a coin or doing the opposite of their instincts.'),
    cq('Bill Hwang\'s Archegos Capital used total return swaps at 5-8x leverage to control $50-80 billion in concentrated positions. When ViacomCBS dropped, $10 billion was destroyed in 48 hours. Which overconfidence biases contributed?',
      ['Only the hot hand fallacy, since the single driver of the collapse was Hwang reading his winning streak as durable skill','Only the illusion of control, because the entire problem was simply Hwang believing he could steer outcomes he could not','All of the above: overestimation (believing his analysis was correct), overplacement (believing he was smarter than the market), overprecision (certainty about concentrated positions), hot hand fallacy (increasing leverage after wins), and illusion of control (using swaps to hide positions)','Hwang was not overconfident at all, he was simply unlucky that a routine stock offering happened to hit his positions'],
      2,'Archegos is the most comprehensive case study of overconfidence ever observed. Every form of overconfidence was present: overestimation (8 stocks can\'t all be right), overplacement (I\'m smarter than every other market participant), overprecision (5x leverage means zero tolerance for being wrong), hot hand fallacy (each winning year increased leverage), and illusion of control (swaps hid positions from regulators). The $10B loss was not bad luck, it was the mathematically inevitable consequence of maximum overconfidence.'),
    cq('Paul Slovic\'s research gave horse racing handicappers varying amounts of information (5, 10, 20, 40 pieces). What happened to their accuracy and confidence as information increased?',
      ['Both accuracy and confidence rose together in proportion, so the extra data genuinely improved the handicappers\' picks','Accuracy rose steadily but confidence stayed flat, meaning the handicappers used the new information without feeling surer','Accuracy stayed flat at ~17% while confidence doubled from 17% to 34%, proving that more information creates the illusion of knowledge, not actual knowledge','Both accuracy and confidence fell as information increased, because the extra data simply overwhelmed and confused the handicappers'],
      2,'This is one of the most important findings in behavioral finance. Information increases confidence but not accuracy. Applied to investing: subscribing to more newsletters, watching more CNBC, and reading more analyst reports makes you feel more informed, but doesn\'t improve your predictions. The illusion of knowledge is a direct driver of overconfidence, which is a direct driver of excessive trading, which Odean proved destroys returns.'),
    cq('The SPIVA Scorecard consistently shows that over 15 years, approximately what percentage of large-cap active fund managers underperform the S&P 500?',
      ['50%, it\'s roughly a coin flip','70%','92%','99%'],
      2,'92% of large-cap active managers underperform over 15 years, and ~95% underperform over 20 years. These are professionals with Bloomberg terminals, analyst teams, advanced degrees, and decades of experience. If they can\'t beat a simple index fund charging 0.03% in fees, the implication for overconfident amateur stock pickers is clear: the most profitable investment decision most people can make is accepting that they cannot beat the market and investing in a low-cost index fund.'),
    cq('A novice trader makes 5 profitable trades in a row and declares "I\'ve figured out the market." On the Dunning-Kruger curve, where is this person, and what does the hot hand fallacy predict will happen next?',
      ['They are on the Slope of Enlightenment and will keep steadily improving, since five wins prove they are already past the beginner stage','They\'re at the peak of Mount Stupid, maximum confidence with minimum competence. The hot hand fallacy makes them interpret a random streak as skill, leading to increased risk-taking. The most likely outcome: escalating bets followed by a devastating loss when the streak inevitably ends.','They are stuck in the Valley of Despair, where their confidence has fallen too low for them to keep trading effectively','Five winning trades is genuinely enough to confirm real skill, so scaling up the position size at this point is fully justified'],
      1,'Five profitable trades is well within random chance, even a coin flip produces 5 heads 3.1% of the time. The Dunning-Kruger effect places this novice at Mount Stupid: they lack the experience to recognize how little they know. The hot hand fallacy converts this random streak into a false skill signal, leading to risk escalation. The historical pattern is predictable: escalating bets → inevitable adverse event → catastrophic loss. This is the Archegos pattern at the retail level.')
