            { label: 'Keep the vacation fund, you\'ve earned this trip and the accounts are separate', consequence: 'Your mental accounting system treats these as two separate situations. Rationally, they are one: you have $0 net and are paying $1,760 per year (22% x $8,000) for the illusion of having savings. The vacation "fund" is actually funded by credit card interest.', score: 0 },
            { label: 'Pay off the credit card debt with the vacation fund. This is a guaranteed 22% return. Rebuild the vacation fund from the $1,760 annual savings. The vacation is delayed, but your net wealth improves by $1,760 per year.', consequence: 'You defeated mental accounting by seeing both accounts as a single balance sheet. Paying off 23% debt with 1% savings is a guaranteed 22% return, the best investment available. You can rebuild the vacation fund quickly from the interest savings. Delayed gratification creates permanent wealth.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'Pay off half the debt, a compromise', consequence: 'Half-measures leave $4,000 at 23%, costing $920/year in unnecessary interest. If paying off the full debt is rational (it is), doing half is half as rational. Mental accounting is negotiating with your reason.', score: 1 }
