            { label: 'Sell. The $6,000 loss already exists. The drug trial failure invalidated your thesis. Apply the "fresh money" test: would you invest $4,000 in this company today? The answer is clearly no. Redeploy the $4,000 to better opportunities and harvest the tax loss.', consequence: 'Outstanding. You defeated the sunk cost fallacy by recognizing the loss already exists, applied the fresh money test to remove emotional attachment, and identified the tax loss harvesting benefit. The $4,000 is now freed to earn returns elsewhere, and the $6,000 loss provides a tax deduction. Selling the losing position actually improves your financial situation on multiple dimensions.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'Average down, buy more at $20 to reduce your cost basis', consequence: 'Averaging down after a thesis-breaking event (failed drug trial) is throwing good money after bad, the sunk cost fallacy escalating. Your cost basis is a mental accounting artifact; the market doesn\'t care what you paid. The drug failed. The thesis is broken. Adding capital is compounding the error.', score: 0 }
