      'Paying off high-interest debt with low-interest savings, selling broken-thesis losers with the fresh money test, rejecting the house money effect on gains, and treating tax refunds as salary with optimal allocation, scoring 12/12.',
      'Mental accounting is the invisible architecture of every financial decision you make. It determines which money you save, which you spend, which you risk, and which you protect, but its categories are arbitrary and often irrational. A dollar is a dollar is a dollar, regardless of whether it came from a paycheck, a refund, a bonus, or an investment gain. The antidote is radical fungibility: before every financial decision, ask "Would I make this same decision if this money came from a different source?" If not, mental accounting is driving, and you need to take the wheel.'
