      {label:'Add a pop-up warning every time users open the app saying "Frequent checking hurts returns"',outcome:'Warning pop-ups are annoying and often dismissed. Research shows that informational nudges alone are less effective than structural nudges (changing defaults). A pop-up is better than nothing but addresses the symptom (checking) rather than the architecture (what the app shows by default).',correct:false},
      {label:'Default to showing long-term performance (1-year, 5-year) on the home screen instead of daily changes, reduce notification frequency to monthly, and make daily view accessible but not the default, designing the architecture to discourage myopic loss aversion while preserving the freedom to check anytime',outcome:'Perfect nudge design. You\'re applying Benartzi and Thaler\'s myopic loss aversion insight: less frequent checking reduces the subjective pain of loss aversion. By making the long-term view the default and monthly notifications the standard, you harness inertia to reduce harmful overtrading. The daily view remains available for anyone who wants it, preserving liberty while changing the architecture.',correct:true},
      {label:'Remove the ability to check portfolios more than once per month',outcome:'This violates the core principle of nudge theory: freedom of choice must be preserved. Removing options is a mandate, not a nudge. Investors should always have access to their data, the nudge should be in what they see FIRST (default view) and how often they\'re prompted (notification frequency), not in restricting access.',correct:false}
