            { label: 'Maintain a decision journal: record every decision BEFORE the outcome is known (thesis, alternatives, emotional state, kill criteria). Review quarterly to identify systematic patterns in your decision-making process, independent of outcomes.', consequence: 'Perfect. The decision journal is the long-term learning engine of the framework. After 50+ entries, patterns emerge that are invisible in the moment: "I trade more during volatile weeks" or "My best decisions come after consulting my accountability partner." Reviewing process quality separately from outcome quality is the key to continuous improvement.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'Read investing books whenever performance is bad', consequence: 'Reactive learning (reading books after losses) is itself a behavioral pattern, it\'s seeking comfort during emotional distress rather than systematically improving. The decision journal enables PROACTIVE learning, identifying process weaknesses before they cause the next loss. Learning should be continuous and process-focused, not reactive and outcome-triggered.', score: 0 }
