    cq('Kahneman said: "I\'ve been studying biases for 45 years and I still fall for them." What is the most important implication of this statement for investors?',
      ['Behavioral finance is useless, because if even the world\'s leading expert still falls for biases after 45 years, then studying them clearly cannot help an ordinary investor avoid them either','Knowledge of biases is necessary but insufficient, the solution is building SYSTEMS (IPS, checklists, circuit breakers, accountability partners) that make rational decisions automatic, because no amount of knowledge can overcome hardwired cognitive patterns in real-time emotional situations','Investors should give up on thinking entirely and just buy index funds, since biases are unbeatable and passive investing is the only safe response to the limits of human judgment','Kahneman must simply be a poor investor, and his honest admission proves that the people who study these biases are actually worse at managing money than everyone else'],
      1,'This is the foundational insight of the entire rational framework approach. If the world\'s foremost expert on cognitive biases still falls for them, knowledge alone clearly isn\'t enough. The solution is architectural: build systems that make the rational choice the default, use checklists to force System 2 engagement, implement circuit breakers to pause during emotional moments, and recruit accountability partners to provide external rationality. Systems replace willpower, and willpower always fails during market extremes.'),
    cq('Atul Gawande showed checklists reduced surgical deaths by 47%. Mohnish Pabrai uses a 70-point investment checklist. Why are checklists so powerful for decision-making?',
      ['They simply make people feel more organized and in control, giving a comforting sense of structure even though the act of ticking boxes does not actually change the quality of the underlying decision','Checklists force System 2 engagement by requiring specific analytical steps that cannot be skipped, they externalize the decision process from memory (which fails under stress) to a physical document (which is reliable). They also catch low-probability but high-impact errors that experience alone misses, the same reason pilots use checklists despite thousands of hours of experience.','They are only useful for simple, routine decisions, and an experienced investor evaluating a complex company is far better off relying on their intuition than on a rigid list of items','They actually slow decision-making down too much, and in fast-moving markets the time spent working through a long checklist causes investors to miss good opportunities before they can act'],
      1,'Checklists work because they solve three cognitive problems simultaneously: (1) Memory failure, under stress, humans forget critical steps. A checklist externalizes memory. (2) Bias blindness, checklists include explicit bias checks ("Am I anchored? Have I sought disconfirming evidence?") that force confrontation with biases that operate below awareness. (3) Overconfidence, experienced people skip steps because they "know" what they\'re doing. Pabrai\'s 70-point checklist forces him to check every item regardless of how confident he feels, and confidence is often the moment biases are strongest.'),
    cq('An investor\'s decision journal reveals that 80% of their profitable trades involved consulting their accountability partner, while 80% of their losing trades were made alone. What should they do?',
      ['Find a brand new accountability partner, because the current one still allows 20% of consulted trades to lose money, and a better partner would presumably push that loss rate even lower','Add a mandatory rule to their IPS: "No trade exceeding $X shall be executed without first discussing the thesis with my accountability partner." The journal revealed a clear pattern, solo decisions are systematically worse, and the framework should be updated to make the successful behavior (consulting the partner) the default for all significant trades.','Stop investing entirely, since the journal shows the investor cannot be trusted to make trades alone and the safest response to a pattern of losses is to leave the market for good','Ignore the pattern as pure coincidence, since 80% is not a large enough sample to mean anything and the link between consulting a partner and winning trades is probably just luck'],
      1,'This is the decision journal working exactly as designed, revealing a systematic pattern that was invisible in real-time. The 80/20 split is far too strong to be coincidence: the accountability partner provides external rationality that catches biases the investor can\'t see alone. The correct response is to make the successful pattern mandatory: add an IPS rule requiring partner consultation before all significant trades. The framework evolves based on journal evidence, this is continuous improvement in action.'),
    cq('Charlie Munger\'s "inversion" technique asks: "What would guarantee failure?" then avoids those things. Applied to investing, which of these is the best example of inversion?',
      ['Ask "how do I find the single best stock?" and then research dozens of companies in extreme depth until you are confident you have identified the one investment that will outperform every other','Ask "what are the most common ways investors destroy wealth?", then build explicit rules against each one: "I will not panic sell during crashes (loss aversion), I will not chase recent winners (recency bias), I will not concentrate in a single stock (overconfidence), I will not make trades during extreme market emotions (herd mentality)"','Read Warren Buffett\'s annual shareholder letters from cover to cover, copying his exact reasoning and stock picks so that your portfolio mirrors the choices of the most successful investor alive','Study only the companies that have already succeeded spectacularly, learning everything about the winners so that you can recognize the same traits in the next batch of future market leaders'],
      1,'Inversion flips the question from "how do I succeed?" to "what guarantees failure?" Munger\'s insight is that avoiding catastrophic errors is more valuable than finding brilliant opportunities, because errors compound and opportunities are rare. By cataloguing the specific behavioral mistakes that destroy wealth (panic selling, performance chasing, overconcentration, herd following) and building explicit IPS rules against each one, you systematically eliminate the highest-probability paths to failure.'),
    cq('Why is automated dollar-cost averaging into index funds considered the "ultimate" pre-commitment strategy?',
      ['It guarantees the highest possible returns, because buying the same amount every month means you are always invested and never miss the market\'s best days, which mathematically maximizes long-term growth','It completely eliminates the human decision point, no bias can affect a decision that is never made by a human. Loss aversion can\'t cause panic selling because there\'s no sell decision. Overconfidence can\'t cause overtrading because there\'s no trading decision. Herd mentality can\'t cause performance chasing because the allocation never changes. The automation bypasses every cognitive bias simultaneously by removing the cognitive process entirely.','It is simply the cheapest strategy available, because automating contributions removes the need to pay an advisor and index funds carry the lowest fees of any investment option on the market','Index funds always outperform actively managed funds, so automating purchases into them locks in a guaranteed performance edge that no stock-picking investor could ever hope to consistently match'],
      1,'Automated DCA into index funds is the only investment strategy that is literally immune to cognitive biases, because it removes the human from the execution loop. Every other strategy requires a human decision at some point, and every human decision is vulnerable to bias. Automated investing buys during crashes (when loss aversion screams to sell), buys during booms (when overconfidence screams to buy more of the hot thing), and maintains the allocation through every emotional extreme. It\'s not the most exciting strategy, but it\'s the most behaviorally robust.')
