            { label: 'Hold (or even add). The clean slate test says: "Would I buy this at $12,000 given $15,000 fair value?" Yes, it\'s 20% undervalued with strong earnings. The $8,000 sunk cost loss is irrelevant. This is one of those cases where holding is the RATIONAL decision, not the sunk cost decision.', consequence: 'Perfect. You applied the clean slate test correctly: forward-looking analysis supports holding at $12,000 when fair value is $15,000. This demonstrates that the clean slate test doesn\'t always say "sell", it says "make the decision you would make without the baggage of past losses." Sometimes that means hold or buy more.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'Sell half to "reduce risk" from the losing position', consequence: 'Splitting the baby is often a compromise between sunk cost emotion and rational analysis. If the stock is worth $15,000 and you own $12,000, the clean slate test says hold the full position. Selling half forfeits $1,500 in expected value gain.', score: 1 }
