    cq('Sweden switched its national pension from opt-in to opt-out enrollment. Participation changed from 62% to 97%. What does this prove about status quo bias and defaults?',
      ['Swedish citizens are more financially responsible','The 35-point swing proves that defaults, not preferences, drive participation. The same citizens with the same pension plan made dramatically different choices based solely on which option was the default. Status quo bias means most people accept whatever default is presented, making the DESIGN of defaults the most consequential financial decision.','The opt-out system was coercive','Education about pensions improved simultaneously'],
      1,'The Swedish pension switch is the cleanest natural experiment on defaults in finance. Nothing changed except which option was the default. Yet 35% more citizens saved for retirement. This proves that status quo bias, not active choice, determines financial behavior for the majority of people.'),
    cq('Thaler and Benartzi\'s Save More Tomorrow program tripled savings rates from 3.5% to 13.6%. What made this intervention so much more effective than simply asking employees to save more?',
      ['It paid employees to save','It harnessed inertia instead of fighting it. Asking employees to save more NOW triggers loss aversion (less take-home pay). Save More Tomorrow committed them to save more from FUTURE raises, no current sacrifice. Once enrolled, inertia kept the escalation going automatically. 78% participated because the initial commitment was painless and the ongoing execution was effortless.','It provided better investment options','It was mandatory'],
      1,'Save More Tomorrow succeeded because it worked WITH human psychology instead of against it. Traditional "save more" advice fights loss aversion (less money now) and requires ongoing willpower. SMarT committed people to a future action (painless) and let inertia maintain the commitment (effortless). The 78% participation rate vs. typical 10-20% for voluntary savings increases proves the power of designed defaults.'),
    cq('Madrian and Shea found that 76% of auto-enrolled 401(k) participants never changed their default settings. What are the implications for individual investors?',
      ['This means auto-enrollment doesn\'t work','The 76% figure proves that the CHOICE of default determines the investment trajectory for three-quarters of participants. Implications: (1) If your company\'s default is a money market fund, you\'re likely losing $300K+ over your career to inertia. (2) Individual investors must actively audit their defaults rather than accepting them. (3) The most impactful financial action is often changing a single default setting, not making sophisticated investment decisions.','People are lazy and should be forced to choose','Auto-enrollment should be banned because people don\'t actively choose'],
      1,'The 76% figure is the most important statistic in retirement planning. It means that for three-quarters of employees, the 5-minute decision of "which default fund" matters more than decades of subsequent financial decisions, because they\'ll never change it. This is why the shift from money market QDIAs to target-date QDIAs was one of the most consequential regulatory changes in retirement savings history.'),
    cq('The "loyalty penalty" in financial services costs long-term customers 15-30% more than switchers. Using status quo bias, explain why this penalty persists and how to overcome it.',
      ['Long-term customers pay more because they get better service','The loyalty penalty persists because status quo bias makes switching feel risky and effortful while staying feels safe and easy, even when staying costs 15-30% more. Banks, insurers, and advisors rely on this inertia to maintain pricing power over existing customers. The antidote is the annual default audit: systematically compare your current providers to alternatives using specific dollar figures, making the cost of inertia visible and concrete.','Switching providers is always risky','Companies reward loyalty with discounts'],
      1,'The loyalty penalty is status quo bias monetized by financial institutions. They know that inertia keeps customers in place, so they offer competitive rates to attract new customers while gradually increasing costs for existing ones. The annual default audit defeats this by making the dollar cost of staying visible. When you see "$4,200/year in excess advisor fees," the abstract discomfort of switching becomes rational action.'),
    cq('Two identical employees start at different companies on the same day. Company A auto-enrolls at 6% in a target-date fund with auto-escalation. Company B requires opt-in enrollment with a money market default. After 40 years, what is the approximate wealth gap, and what caused it?',
      ['No gap, both employees would optimize their own settings','The gap is approximately $1 million or more. The Company A employee, carried by beneficial defaults (target-date returns, auto-escalation to 10%+, full employer matching captured), accumulates approximately $1.8M. The Company B employee, trapped by harmful defaults (no enrollment for years, then money market returns, no escalation), accumulates approximately $450K. The cause: one checkbox, auto-enrollment, determined which direction inertia pushed for 40 years.','$50,000, defaults have a minor effect','$100,000, over a short period'],
      1,'The million-dollar gap from a single default setting is the most powerful illustration of status quo bias in finance. Neither employee made active "good" or "bad" decisions, inertia did the work. Auto-enrollment, target-date funds, auto-escalation, and full match capture vs. delayed enrollment, money market returns, static contributions, and forfeited matching. The default determined the outcome. This is why behavioral economists argue that default design is the most important financial policy lever available.')
