        { date: 'August 2019', event: 'WeWork files its S-1 (IPO prospectus). Investors read the financial data for the first time: $1.8 billion revenue, $1.6 billion in net losses, and the word "community" appears 150 times.', context: 'The S-1 was the moment narrative met data. The story was compelling; the numbers were catastrophic. Revenue of $1.8B didn\'t cover losses of $1.6B. The business model, long-term leases with short-term subletting, created massive downside risk in any recession. But for 9 years, the narrative had insulated WeWork from fundamental analysis.' },
        { date: 'September 2019', event: 'CEO Adam Neumann is forced out. The IPO is withdrawn. Valuation collapses from $47 billion to approximately $8 billion in weeks.', context: 'When the narrative collapsed (CEO scandal + S-1 data), the emperor had no clothes. A $47B "technology platform" was revealed to be a money-losing real estate company. The 83% valuation decline represented the "narrative premium", the difference between what the story was worth and what the data was worth.' },
        { date: '2023', event: 'WeWork files for Chapter 11 bankruptcy. The company that was once valued at $47 billion is effectively worth $0.', context: 'The final act of the narrative fallacy. A compelling story about "transforming work" attracted $11.5 billion in total funding, all of which was destroyed. The data (unsustainable losses, recession-vulnerable model) was available throughout, but the narrative was so compelling that investors, journalists, and employees suspended disbelief for nearly a decade.' }
